115:The uniqueness of an optimal solution to a combinatorial optimization problem attracts many fields of researchers' attention because it has a wide range of applications, it is related to important classes in computational complexity, and an instance with only one solution is often critical for algorithm designs in theory. However, as the authors know, 
116:there is no major benchmark set consisting of only instances with unique solutions, and no algorithm generating instances with unique solutions is known; %since the (non-)diversity of solutions is an upcoming optimization target, 
117:a systematic approach to getting a problem instance guaranteed having a unique solution would be helpful. A possible approach is as follows: Given a problem instance, we specify a small part of a solution in advance so that only one optimal solution meets the specification. This paper formulates such a ``pre-assignment'' approach for the
122:the problem becomes NP-complete when an input graph is bipartite. 
123:We then present an $O(2.1996^n)$-time algorithm for general graphs and an $O(1.9181^n)$-time algorithm for bipartite graphs, where $n$ is the number of vertices. The latter is based on an FPT algorithm with $O^*(3.6791^{\tau})$ time for vertex cover number $\tau$. Furthermore, we show that the problem for trees can be solved in $O(1.4143^n)$ time.
126:\section{Introduction}
128:Preparing a good benchmark set is indispensable for evaluating the actual performance of problem solvers, such as SAT solvers, combinatorial optimization solvers, and learning algorithms. 
135:In this paper, we consider generating problem instances with unique solutions. It is pointed out that the ``unique solution'' is related to the nature of intractability in SAT. Indeed, PPSZ, currently the fastest SAT algorithm in theory~\cite{scheder2022ppsz}, is based on the unique k-SAT solver. Furthermore, a type of problem that determines whether or not it has a unique solution (e.g., \textsc{Unique SAT}) belongs to a class above NP and coNP~\cite{BlassEt:USAT}, but the status of that class is still not well understood, where an exception is TSP; \textsc{Unique TSP} is known to be $\Delta^p_2$-complete~\cite{Papadimitriou:UOTSP}. 
136:Thus, it is desirable to have a sufficient number of instances with unique solutions in many cases, such as when problem instances with unique solutions are used as benchmarks or when we evaluate the performance of a solver for the uniqueness check version of combinatorial optimization problems. In other words, instances with unique solutions have an important role in experimental studies of computational complexity theory. 
138:In this paper, we focus on \textsc{Vertex Cover} problem as a representative of combinatorial optimization problems (also referred Drosophila in the field of parameterized algorithms~\cite{downey2013fundamentals}), propose a ``pre-assignment'' model as an instance generation model for this problem, and consider the computational complexity of instance generation based on this model. One of the crucial points in generating problem instances with unique solutions is that it is difficult for unweighted combinatorial optimization problems to apply approaches used in conventional generation methods. A simple and conventional way of generating instances is a random generation, but the probability that a randomly generated problem instance has a unique solution is low. Furthermore, although some combinatorial problems have randomized algorithms modifying an instance into an instance with a unique solution, they strongly depend on the structure of the problems, and it is non-trivial to apply them to other problems, including \textsc{Vertex Cover}~\cite{valiantV1986NP,MulmuleyVV1987isolation}. 
140:Besides, the planted model used in SAT instance generation requires guaranteeing the non-existence of solutions other than the assumed solution, which is also a non-trivial task, or some planted instances are shown to be easy to solve~\cite{KrivelevichV06}. Thus, generating instances with unique solutions requires a different approach than conventional instance generation methods. 
142:A pre-assignment for \emph{uniquification} in a problem means assigning an arbitrary part of the decision variables in the problem so that only one solution is consistent with the assignment. In SAT, for example, we select some of the Boolean variables and assign true or false to each so that only one truth assignment consistent with the pre-assignment satisfies the formula. In the case of \textsc{Vertex Cover}, we 
143:select some vertices and assign a role to each, i.e.,  a cover vertex or a non-cover vertex, so that only one minimum vertex cover of the graph is consistent with the pre-assignment.  
144:Such a pre-assignment for uniquification enables the generation of problem instances with unique solutions to combinatorial optimization problems. In the case of \textsc{Vertex Cover}, the graph obtained by the pre-assignment (see \Cref{obs:reduction} for the detail) has only one vertex cover with the minimum size, which can be used as an instance of \textsc{Unique Vertex Cover} problem. 
145:% the graph by the pre-assignment (i.e., the graph obtained by excluding the vertices included in the cover, as well as the adjacent vertices of the vertices not included in the cover) has only one vertex cover with the minimum size, which can be used as an instance of \textsc{Unique Vertex Cover} problem. 
146:A strong point of the pre-assignment-based instance generation is that it transforms an instance of an ordinary problem into an instance of the uniqueness check version of the corresponding problem; if the ordinary version of the problem has a rich benchmark instance set, we can expect to obtain the same amount of instance set by applying the pre-assignment models. Indeed, many papers study instance generations of the ordinary combinatorial optimization problem as seen later; they can be translated into the results of instances with unique solutions. 
148:From this perspective, we formulate the pre-assignment problem to \emph{uniquify} an optimal solution for the vertex cover problem instance. We name the problem \pauvc, 
150:We consider three types of scenarios of pre-assignment: \textsc{Include}, \textsc{Exclude}, and \textsc{Mixed}. %To specify the scenarios, 
151:Under these models, this study aims to determine the computational complexity of the pre-assignment for uniquification of \textsc{Vertex Cover}.
153:\subsection{Our results}
154:We investigate the complexity of \pauvc for all pre-assignment models. 
155:As hardness results, we show:
159:    \item \pauvc for bipartite graphs is NP-complete under any model.  
162:For the positive side, we design exact exponential-time algorithms and FPT algorithms for the vertex cover number. 
166:\item an $O(2.1996^n)$-time algorithm, which works for any model, 
167:\item an $O^*(3.6791^{\tau})$-time algorithm under \textsc{Include} model,\footnote{The $O^*$ notation suppresses polynomial factors in $n$.} 
168:\item an $O^*(3.6791^{\tau})$-time algorithm under \textsc{Mixed} and \textsc{Exclude} models,  
171:Due to the FPT algorithms, \pauvc for bipartite graphs can be solved in $O(1.9181^n)$ time. Here, it should be noted that the fourth and fifth algorithms work in different ways, though they have the same running time.
173:The last results are for trees. Many graph problems are intractable for general graphs but polynomially solvable for trees. Most of such problems are just NP-complete, but some are even PSPACE-complete~\cite{demaine2015linear}. 
175:Thus, many readers might consider that \pauvc for trees is likely solvable in polynomial time. On the other hand, not a few problems (e.g., \textsc{Node Kayles}) are intractable in general, but the time complexity for trees still remains open, and only exponential-time algorithms are known~\cite{BodlaenderKT15,yoshiwatari2022winnerc}. In the case of \pauvc, no polynomial-time algorithm for trees is currently known. Instead, we give an exponential upper bound of the time complexity of \pauvc for trees. 
177:\item an $O(1.4143^n)$-time algorithm for trees under \textsc{Include} model, and 
178:\item an $O(1.4143^n)$-time algorithm for trees under \textsc{Mixed} and \textsc{Exclude} models. 
180:These algorithms also work differently, though they have the same running time.
184:In the context of instance generation, these results imply that the pre-assignment approach could not be universally promising but would work well to generate instances with small vertex covers.  
185:\subsection{Related work}
186:Instance generation in combinatorial (optimization) problems is well-studied from several points of view, such as controlling some attributes~\cite{asahiro1996random} and hard instance generation~\cite{horie1997hard}, and some empirical studies use such generated instances for performance evaluation~\cite{cha1995performance}. 
187:Such instances are desirable to be sufficiently hard because they are supposed to be used for practical performance evaluation of algorithms for problems considered hard in computational complexity theory (e.g., ~\cite{sanchis1995generating,neuen2017benchmark,mccreesh2018subgraph}). 
188:Other than these, many studies present empirically practical instance generators (e.g., \cite{ullrich2018generic}). From the computational complexity side, it is shown that, unless NP $=$ coNP, for most NP-hard problems, there exists no polynomial-time algorithm capable of generating all instances of the problem, with known answers~\cite{sanchis1990complexity,matsuyama2021hardness}. 
191:%As mentioned in the previous section, our pre-assignment problem is an FCP-like problem. 
192:A natural application of pre-assignment for uniquification is puzzle instance generation. In pencil puzzles such as \textsc{Sudoku}, an instance is supposed to have a unique solution as the answer. 
195:If there is a unique satisfying assignment $f_X: X \to \{0, 1\}$ extending $f_Y$, that is, $f_Y(x) = f_X(x)$ for $x \in Y$, we call variables in $Y$ \emph{clues}.
196:The FCP-SAT problem asks, given a CNF formula $\phi$ and an integer $k$, whether $\phi$ has a unique satisfying assignment with at most $k$ clues.
197:%This problem naturally extends the Unique SAT problem, as it asks whether the empty clue leads to a unique satisfying assignment. 
202:%They also show that FCP 2-SAT is NP-complete, whereas the original version of the 2-SAT problem is solvable in polynomial time. 
206:The uniqueness of an (optimal) solution itself has been intensively and extensively studied for many combinatorial optimization problems. Some of them are shown to have the same time complexity to \textsc{Unique SAT}~\cite{hudry2019complexity,DBLP:journals/tcs/HudryL19}, while \textsc{Unique TSP} is shown to be $\Delta^P_2$-complete~\cite{Papadimitriou:UOTSP}. Juban \cite{Juban99} provides the dichotomy theorem for checking whether there
207:exists a unique solution to a given propositional formula. The theorem partitions the instances of the
208:problem between the polynomial-time solvable and coNP-hard cases, where Horn, anti-Horn, affine, and 2SAT formulas are the only polynomial-time solvable cases. 
212:%Solution uniqueness also appears in several pencil-and-paper puzzles, such as the well-known Sudoku puzzle. 
215:%have distinct numbers. Initial configurations are usually designed in such a way that the (completely filled) solution is uniquely determined. Moreover, in order to make difficult/fun initial configurations, clues (i.e., filled cells in the initial configuration) should be minimized as much as possible. 
220:\section{Preliminaries}\label{sec:preli}
221:\subsection{Notations}
239:\subsection{Models}\label{sec:model}
240:In \pauvc, given a graph $G$ and an integer $k$, the goal is to determine whether $G$ has a ``feasible pre-assignment of size at most $k$''.
242:Under \textsc{Include} model, a pre-assignment is defined as a vertex subset $U \subseteq V(G)$, and a pre-assignment $\tilde{U}$ is said to be \emph{feasible} if there is a minimum vertex cover $U^*$ of $G$ (i.e., $\tau(G) = |U^*|$) such that $U \subseteq U^*$ and for every other minimum vertex cover $U$ of $G$, $\tilde{U} \setminus U$ is nonempty.
243:In other words, $\tilde{U}$ is feasible if there is a unique minimum vertex cover $U^*$ of $G$ that includes $\tilde{U}$ (i.e., $\tilde{U} \subseteq U^*$).
244:Under \textsc{Exclude} model, a pre-assignment is also defined as a vertex subset $\tilde{U} \subseteq V(G)$ as well as \textsc{Include}, and $\tilde{U}$ is said to be feasible if there is a unique minimum vertex cover $U^*$ of $G$ that excludes $X$ (i.e., $U^* \subseteq V(G) \setminus \tilde{U}$).
245:For these two models, the size of a pre-assignment is defined as its cardinality.
246:Under \textsc{Mixed} model, a pre-assignment is defined as a pair of vertex subsets $(\tilde{U}_{\rm in}, \tilde{U}_{\rm ex})$, and $(\tilde{U}_{\rm in}, \tilde{U}_{\rm ex})$ is said to be feasible if there is a unique minimum vertex cover $U^*$ of $G$ that includes $\tilde{U}_{\rm in}$ and excludes $\tilde{U}_{\rm ex}$ (i.e., $\tilde{U}_{\rm in} \subseteq U^* \subseteq V(G) \setminus \tilde{U}_{\rm ex}$).
247:We can assume that $\tilde{U}_{\rm in}$ and $\tilde{U}_{\rm ex}$ are disjoint, as otherwise the pre-assignment is trivially infeasible.
248:The size of the pre-assignment $(\tilde{U}_{\rm in}, \tilde{U}_{\rm ex})$ is defined as $|\tilde{U}_{\rm in}| + |\tilde{U}_{\rm ex}|$ under the \textsc{Mixed} model.
250:    Let $G$ be a graph and let $(\tilde{U}_{\rm in}, \tilde{U}_{\rm ex})$ be a feasible pre-assignment of $G$.
251:    Then, $G-(\tilde{U}_{\rm in} \cup N(\tilde{U}_{\rm ex}))$ has a unique minimum vertex cover of size $\tau(G) - |\tilde{U}_{\rm in} \cup N(\tilde{U}_{\rm ex})|$.
256:\begin{theorem}\label{thm:include-and-exclude}
257:    If $G$ has a feasible pre-assignment of size at most $k$ in the \textsc{Include} model, then $G$ has a feasible pre-assignment of size at most $k$ under the \textsc{Exclude} model.
261:    Let $\tilde{U} \subseteq V(G)$ be a feasible pre-assignment for $G$ in the \textsc{Include} model.
262:    Let $U^* \subseteq V(G)$ be the unique minimum vertex cover of $G$ such that $\tilde{U} \subseteq U^*$.
267:    We claim that $\tilde{U}'$ is a feasible pre-assignment of $G$ in the \textsc{Exclude} model.
270:    To see the uniqueness of $U^*$ (under the pre-assignment $\tilde{U}'$ in \textsc{Exclude}), suppose that there is a minimum vertex cover $U$ of $G$ with $U \neq U^*$ such that $U \subseteq V(G) \setminus \tilde{U}'$.
272:    Thus, we have $\tilde{U} \subseteq U$, contradicting the uniqueness of $U^*$. By $|\tilde{U}'| \le |\tilde{U}|$, the theorem holds.
278:Under \textsc{Exclude} model, a pre-assignment containing exactly one vertex is feasible for $K_n$.
279:However, under \textsc{Include} model, any pre-assignment of at most $n - 2$ vertices does not uniquify a minimum vertex cover of $K_n$.
283:\begin{theorem}\label{thm:mix-and-exclude}
284:    A graph $G$ has a feasible pre-assignment of size at most $k$ under the \textsc{Exclude} model if and only if $G$ has a feasible pre-assignment of size at most $k$ under the \textsc{Mixed} model.
287:    The forward implication follows from the observation that for any feasible pre-assignment $\tilde{U}$ of $G$ in the \textsc{Exclude} model, the pair $(\emptyset, \tilde{U})$ is a feasible pre-assignment of $G$ in the \textsc{Mixed} model.
290:    Let $(\tilde{U}_{\rm in}, \tilde{U}_{\rm ex})$ be a feasible pre-assignment of $G$ under the \textsc{Mixed} model.
291:    Let $U^* \subseteq V(G)$ be the unique vertex cover of $G$ such that $\tilde{U}_{\rm in} \subseteq U^* \subseteq V(G) \setminus \tilde{U}_{\rm ex}$.
294:    We claim that $\tilde{U}$ is a feasible pre-assignment of $G$ under the \textsc{Exclude} model.
297:    To see the uniqueness of $U^*$, suppose that there is a minimum vertex cover $U$ of $G$ with $U \neq U^*$ such that $U \subseteq V(G) \setminus \tilde{U}$.
300:    Thus, we have $\tilde{U}_{\rm in} \subseteq U \subseteq V(G) \setminus \tilde{U}_{\rm ex}$, contradicting the uniqueness of $U^*$.
305:\begin{lemma}\label{lem:unique_alg}
307:    Suppose that there is an algorithm $\mathcal A$ for computing a minimum vertex cover of a given graph $G \in \mathcal G$ that runs in time $T(n, m)$, where $n = |V(G)|$ and $m = |E(G)|$.\footnote{We assume that $n + m \le T(n, m) \le T(n', m')$ for $n \le n'$ and $m \le m'$.}
308:    Then, we can check whether $G \in \mathcal G$ has a unique minimum vertex cover in time $O(\tau(G) \cdot T(n, m))$.
312:    If $U$ is not a unique minimum vertex cover of $G$, $G$ has another minimum vertex cover $U'$ not containing $v$ for some $v \in U$.
317:Using~\Cref{lem:unique_alg}, we have the following corollary.
318:\begin{corollary}\label{cor:unique_alg}
320:    Suppose that there is an algorithm for computing a minimum vertex cover of a given graph $G \in \mathcal G$ that runs in time $T(n, m)$, where $n = |V(G)|$ and $m = |E(G)|$.
321:    Given a graph $G \in \mathcal G$ and vertex sets $\tilde{U}_{\rm in}, \tilde{U}_{\rm ex} \subseteq V(G)$, a pre-assignment $(\tilde{U}_{\rm in}, \tilde{U}_{\rm ex})$ is feasible for $G$ under the \textsc{Mixed} model in time $O(\tau(G)\cdot T(n, m))$.
324:    If either $\tilde{U}_{\rm in} \cap \tilde{U}_{\rm ex} \neq \emptyset$ or $\tilde{U}_{\rm ex}$ is not an independent set of $G$, the pre-assignment is trivially infeasible.
327:    Therefore, we can check whether $G$ has a unique minimum vertex cover $U$ satisfying $\tilde{U}_{\rm in} \subseteq U \subseteq V(G) \setminus \tilde{U}_{\rm ex}$ using the algorithm in~\Cref{lem:unique_alg} as well.
330:\section{Complexity}\label{sec:complexity}
331:This section is devoted to proving the complexity of \pauvc. In particular, we show that \pauvc is $\Sigma^P_2$-complete on general graphs and NP-complete on bipartite graphs.
333:\subsection{General graphs}\label{sec:complexity:general}
335:\begin{theorem}\label{thm:sigma}
336:{\rm PAU-VC} is $\Sigma^P_2$-complete under any type of pre-assignment.   
339:% \Cref{thm:sigma} is shown by performing a polynomial-reduction from \textsc{FCP 1-in-3 SAT}, which is known to be $\Sigma_2^P$-complete~\cite{DemaineEt:FCP}.
341:Recall that a decision problem belongs to $\Sigma^P_2$ if and only if it is solvable in polynomial time by a non-deterministic Turing machine augmented by an oracle for an NP-complete problem. We then see that \pauvc belongs to $\Sigma^P_2$, because  
342:under a pre-assignment as a witness we can decide in polynomial time whether an input graph has a unique vertex cover with an NP-oracle as shown in \Cref{cor:unique_alg}.   
344:We then prove the hardness by reducing $\Sigma^P_2$-complete problem \textsc{FCP 1-in-3 SAT} (\cite{DemaineEt:FCP}) to \pauvc.
379:We show that there is a bijection from the solutions of instance $C$ of \textsc{1-in-3 SAT} to the optimal solutions of $G_C$ of \textsc{Vertex Cover}, which are vertex covers with size $k=3n+2m$. The proof is constructive; we give an injective and surjective mapping. Note that we can forget the uniqueness of a solution at this moment. 
430:In \textsc{FCP 1-in-3 SAT}, given an instance $(X, C)$ of \textsc{1-in-3 SAT} and an integer $k$, the goal is to compute a subset $Y \subseteq X$ of at most $k$ variables such that by taking some truth assignment $f_Y$ to $Y$, there is a unique 1-in-3 assignment $f_X$ to $X$ extending $f_Y$, that is, $f_X(x) = f_Y(x)$ for $x \in Y$.
432:There are three models of pre-assignment: \textsc{Include}/\textsc{Exclude}/\textsc{Mixed}. 
436:We first consider the \textsc{Include} model. For a given instance $C$ of \textsc{FCP 1-in-3 SAT}, we construct $G_C$. We claim that $C$ has at most $k$ clues (i.e., a truth assignment for at most $k$ variables) such that the resulting $C$ has a unique solution if and only if $G_C$ has a feasible pre-assignment of size at most $k$. For the if-direction, suppose $X'$ is the set of $k$ variables. 
437:If $x\in X'$ is assigned true (resp., false), we include $v(x)$ (resp., $v(\bar{x})$) in $\tilde{U}$, which makes a pre-assignment with size $k$. The bijection shown above guarantees that a $(3n+2m)$-vertex cover of $G_C$ containing $\tilde{U}$ is unique, that is, the pre-assignment is feasible.
439:For the only-if direction, suppose that $\tilde{U}$ is a feasible pre-assignment for $G_C$ with size at most $k$.
440:Let $U^*$ denote the unique minimum vertex cover of $G_C$ with $\tilde{U} \subseteq U^*$. Note that $\tilde{U}$ should be consistent with the conditions in the claim; for example, $\tilde{U}$ cannot include $v(x)$ and $v(\bar{x})$ simultaneously. 
441:If $\tilde{U}\subseteq V^X_C$, $\tilde{U}$ defines $k$ clues for $C$. Namely, if $v(x)\in \tilde{U}$ (resp., $v(\bar{x})\in \tilde{U}$), $x$ is assigned true (resp., false) in the clues, whose size becomes $k$.  Then the bijection guarantees the uniqueness of the solution under the clues.  
449:Also for this case, we show that $C$ has at most $k$ clues (i.e., a truth assignment for at most $k$ variables) such that the resulting $C$ has a unique solution if and only if $G_C$ has a feasible pre-assignment of size at most $k$. For the if-direction, suppose $X'$ is the set of $k$ variables. 
450:If $x\in X'$ is assigned true (resp., false), we exclude $v(\bar{x})$ (resp., $v({x})$) in $\hat{U}$, which makes a pre-assignment with size $k$. For this case also, the bijection shown above guarantees that $G_C$ has a unique $(3n+2m)$-vertex cover of $G_C$ that does not contain any vertex in $\hat{U}$, that is, the pre-assignment is feasible.
452:For the only-if direction, suppose that $\hat{U}$ is a feasible pre-assignment for $G_C$. Let $U^*$ denote the unique minimum vertex cover of $G_C$ with $U^* \subseteq V(G_C) \setminus \hat{U}$.
453:If $\hat{U}\subseteq V^X_C$, a similar argument to \textsc{Include} defines $k$ clues for $C$, which guarantees the uniqueness of the solution. We then consider the case when $\hat{U}$ contains a vertex outside of $V^X_C$. Such a vertex can be $r$ or one in $V_C^{X'} \cup V_C^{\mathrm{clause}}$. If $r\in \hat{U}$, we can remove it by condition \textbf{2} of the claim. If $v'(l)\in \hat{U}\cap V_C^{X'}$, where $l$ is a literal (i.e., $l=x$ or $l=\bar{x}$ for some $x$), we can replace $v'(l)$ by $v(l)$ in $\hat{U}$ by a similar argument to \textsc{Include} again. 
460:For bipartite graphs, \textsc{Vertex Cover} is equivalent to the problem of finding a maximum cardinality matching by K\"{o}nig's theorem (see Chapter 2 in \cite{Diestel}), which can be computed in polynomial time by the Hopcroft-Karp algorithm~\cite{HopcroftK73}.
461:Combining this fact and \Cref{cor:unique_alg}, \pauvc belongs to NP under both models.
