index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..0f8e2a825028cf6db23bf1091d0084da0ed2c3f9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,196 @@
+# PAU-VC generator for arXiv:2312.10599
+
+**Status:** the generator and local gates G1--G8 pass, but this result is **not
+shippable yet**.  The required oracle run stopped after two genuine failures
+because the shared OpenRouter key reached its total spending limit.  G9 is
+therefore recorded as failed/unavailable, not guessed.
+
+| Profile field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **A — structural hardness** |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | graph |
+| Certificate form | integer tuple (the paper's native vertex set) |
+| Intuition | constraint propagation: a small all-zero cross-block must also dominate the core |
+| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
+| Reduction | paper-licensed, Section 3, Lemmas 4--5 and Theorem 4 |
+
+## Problem and construction
+
+This implements Horiyama, Kobayashi, Ono, Seto, and Suzuki, [*Theoretical
+Aspects of Generating Instances with Unique Solutions: Pre-assignment Models
+for Unique Vertex Cover*](https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.10599).
+
+The solver receives a bipartite core graph `G`, its pendant augmentation `G'`
+(one private leaf for each core vertex), and a budget `k`.  It must return a
+nonempty set `X` of at most `k` core vertices such that exactly one minimum
+vertex cover of `G'` avoids `X`.  On these promised instances, Section 3 proves
+that this is exactly an independent dominating set of `G`: no two vertices of
+`X` are adjacent, and every core vertex is in or adjacent to `X`.  Those two
+local checks make grading exact and cheap.
+
+Generation is inverse.  A balanced set `D` is sampled first.  No edge joins its
+two halves; every decoy receives one random neighbor in the opposite half of
+`D`; and decoy--decoy edges are sampled at the rate that equalises expected
+plant and decoy degrees.  Labels are then uniformly permuted and private leaves
+are attached.  Lemma 4 constructs the unique compatible minimum cover as
+`(V(G)-D)` together with the private mates of `D`; no generated instance is
+solved to obtain its answer.
+
+## Why Track A
+
+Section 3, Theorem 4 proves Exclude/Mixed PAU-VC NP-complete for bipartite
+graphs through this pendant-corona reduction from bipartite Independent
+Dominating Set.  The construction therefore stays in the paper's graph
+objects, although the reduction is representational rather than unrestricted
+native coverage.
+
+The paper also gives the important easy side: Corollary 1 makes verification
+polynomial on bipartite graphs via Hopcroft--Karp; Section 4 gives
+`O(1.9181^N)` time on `N`-vertex bipartite inputs and
+`O*(3.6791^tau)` parameterised by vertex-cover number `tau`.  The augmented
+shipping graph has 320 vertices and `tau=160`, so it is deliberately outside
+the small-`tau` regime the authors identify as favorable.  On eight shipping
+seeds, the included exact independent-domination brancher exhausted one
+million nodes every time (30.71 seconds total) without finding a witness.
+This is distribution evidence, not a proof of average-case hardness.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+The `demo` preset with seed 0 renders the following complete small instance:
+
+```text
+PRE-ASSIGNMENT FOR A UNIQUE MINIMUM VERTEX COVER
+
+Vertices are 0 through 15; k=2.
+CORE W: 0 3 5 6 7 10 12 13
+PRIVATE PAIRS (core leaf):
+0 4
+3 9
+5 15
+6 14
+7 1
+10 11
+12 2
+13 8
+EDGES:
+0 4
+0 5
+0 6
+0 13
+1 7
+2 12
+3 5
+3 6
+3 9
+3 10
+5 12
+5 15
+6 7
+6 14
+7 10
+7 13
+8 13
+10 11
+10 12
+12 13
+```
+
+The answer is `[3, 13]`:
+
+```python
+>>> verify(inst, [3, 13])
+(True, 'ok')
+>>> verify(inst, [3])
+(False, 'selected vertices do not dominate core vertex 0')
+```
+
+A person can solve this demo on paper by checking the 28 core pairs and eight
+closed neighborhoods.  `enumerate_all` finds four accepted witnesses because
+the task intentionally accepts every valid pre-assignment, not only the plant.
+
+## Presets
+
+| Preset | Core `n` | Budget `k` | Plant incidence | Approx. rendered tokens | Ships? |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 8 | 2 | 1 | 366 tokens | no; hand example |
+| easy | 160 | 10 | 1 | 3,221 | intended, pending oracle evidence |
+| medium | 208 | 12 | 1 | 4,404 | no |
+| hard | 264 | 14 | 1 | 6,012 | no |
+
+`SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY` is provisionally `easy`; it must not be submitted until
+the bare and G9 runs complete.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| Gate | Result | Measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 planted verifies | pass | 12/12 preset/seed checks; JSON round-trip included |
+| G2 corruption | pass | 5/5 rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 parser round-trip | pass | model-style tagged response recovered; garbage returned `None` |
+| G4 guess resistance | pass | 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses; language size 2,434,053,505,276,148 |
+| G5 density/baseline | pass | shipping 0/200,000; exact brancher hit 1,000,000-node cap in 3.84 s |
+| G6 adversaries | pass | degree, greedy, 256 restarts, centered spectral, and exact search all 0/8 |
+| G7 scaling | pass | doubled 320-core instance builds and verifies; answer remains length 10 |
+| G8 canonical key | pass | 60/60 relabellings invariant and valid; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9 no-tool | **blocked/fail** | answer 40 chars/10 atoms, route 170 operations; hinted oracle arm unavailable |
+
+The G4 prior is uniform over every nonempty core subset of size at most `k`,
+so it already enforces the stated shape, core-membership, uniqueness, and size
+constraints.  It does not model a solver's learned graph prior.
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+The harness-owned bare transcript is retained exactly as written.  It has no
+verdict because the third non-error attempt could not be made.
+
+| Arm/model | Seed | Outcome | Reason |
+|---|---:|---|---|
+| bare / GPT-5.6 Terra | 902765844 | failed | parsed 10 labels; core vertex 12 was undominated |
+| bare / Claude Sonnet 5 | 1676755929 | failed | empty length-limited response after 32,000 completion tokens |
+| bare / Gemini/Grok retries | various | error, not scored | HTTP 403 key total limit exhausted |
+| hinted | — | 0/0 | not run |
+| placebo | — | 0/0 | not run |
+
+The hinted-minus-placebo statistic is unavailable; the structural hint has not
+yet been tested.  The answer itself is 40 characters, about 10 tokens and 10
+atomic elements.  After identifying a candidate zero block, the intended exact
+route uses 170 set-intersection checks, under the 300-operation cap.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+from gen_2312_10599 import DIFFICULTY, make_instance, render, parse_answer, verify
+
+inst = make_instance(seed=7, **DIFFICULTY["easy"])
+print(render(inst))
+candidate = parse_answer("<answer>1,2,3</answer>")
+print(verify(inst, candidate))
+```
+
+After a fresh OpenRouter quota is available, rerun:
+
+```bash
+cd results/2312.10599
+python3 ../../scripts/harden.py gen_2312_10599.py
+```
+
+Then run structural and placebo copies as prescribed in G9 and finally emit
+from the repository root with `bash scripts/emit.sh 2312.10599`.
+
+## Caveats
+
+- NP-completeness is worst-case; it does not prove this planted distribution
+  hard.  The empirical panel is encouraging but only covers eight seeds.
+- No industrial SAT, CP-SAT, or MILP solver was available under the
+  standard-library-only contract.  The exact brancher and a centered spectral
+  planted-block attack were tested; stronger LP/SDP relaxations were not.
+- The generator guarantees one witness but not uniqueness of the
+  pre-assignment.  This is intentional: the requested witness is *a* feasible
+  pre-assignment, and `verify` accepts alternatives.
+- The Weisfeiler--Lehman key is a strong cheap relabelling invariant for these
+  random marked cores, not a complete graph-isomorphism canonical form.
+- A solver that recovers the planted zero block using a stronger biclustering
+  method could make the distribution easy.  The unfinished four-vendor and G9
+  runs are therefore mandatory before trusting or shipping this family.
