318:\begin{corollary}\label{cor:unique_alg}
320:    Suppose that there is an algorithm for computing a minimum vertex cover of a given graph $G \in \mathcal G$ that runs in time $T(n, m)$, where $n = |V(G)|$ and $m = |E(G)|$.
321:    Given a graph $G \in \mathcal G$ and vertex sets $\tilde{U}_{\rm in}, \tilde{U}_{\rm ex} \subseteq V(G)$, a pre-assignment $(\tilde{U}_{\rm in}, \tilde{U}_{\rm ex})$ is feasible for $G$ under the \textsc{Mixed} model in time $O(\tau(G)\cdot T(n, m))$.
324:    If either $\tilde{U}_{\rm in} \cap \tilde{U}_{\rm ex} \neq \emptyset$ or $\tilde{U}_{\rm ex}$ is not an independent set of $G$, the pre-assignment is trivially infeasible.
327:    Therefore, we can check whether $G$ has a unique minimum vertex cover $U$ satisfying $\tilde{U}_{\rm in} \subseteq U \subseteq V(G) \setminus \tilde{U}_{\rm ex}$ using the algorithm in~\Cref{lem:unique_alg} as well.
331:This section is devoted to proving the complexity of \pauvc. In particular, we show that \pauvc is $\Sigma^P_2$-complete on general graphs and NP-complete on bipartite graphs.
336:{\rm PAU-VC} is $\Sigma^P_2$-complete under any type of pre-assignment.   
339:% \Cref{thm:sigma} is shown by performing a polynomial-reduction from \textsc{FCP 1-in-3 SAT}, which is known to be $\Sigma_2^P$-complete~\cite{DemaineEt:FCP}.
341:Recall that a decision problem belongs to $\Sigma^P_2$ if and only if it is solvable in polynomial time by a non-deterministic Turing machine augmented by an oracle for an NP-complete problem. We then see that \pauvc belongs to $\Sigma^P_2$, because  
342:under a pre-assignment as a witness we can decide in polynomial time whether an input graph has a unique vertex cover with an NP-oracle as shown in \Cref{cor:unique_alg}.   
344:We then prove the hardness by reducing $\Sigma^P_2$-complete problem \textsc{FCP 1-in-3 SAT} (\cite{DemaineEt:FCP}) to \pauvc.
379:We show that there is a bijection from the solutions of instance $C$ of \textsc{1-in-3 SAT} to the optimal solutions of $G_C$ of \textsc{Vertex Cover}, which are vertex covers with size $k=3n+2m$. The proof is constructive; we give an injective and surjective mapping. Note that we can forget the uniqueness of a solution at this moment. 
430:In \textsc{FCP 1-in-3 SAT}, given an instance $(X, C)$ of \textsc{1-in-3 SAT} and an integer $k$, the goal is to compute a subset $Y \subseteq X$ of at most $k$ variables such that by taking some truth assignment $f_Y$ to $Y$, there is a unique 1-in-3 assignment $f_X$ to $X$ extending $f_Y$, that is, $f_X(x) = f_Y(x)$ for $x \in Y$.
432:There are three models of pre-assignment: \textsc{Include}/\textsc{Exclude}/\textsc{Mixed}. 
436:We first consider the \textsc{Include} model. For a given instance $C$ of \textsc{FCP 1-in-3 SAT}, we construct $G_C$. We claim that $C$ has at most $k$ clues (i.e., a truth assignment for at most $k$ variables) such that the resulting $C$ has a unique solution if and only if $G_C$ has a feasible pre-assignment of size at most $k$. For the if-direction, suppose $X'$ is the set of $k$ variables. 
437:If $x\in X'$ is assigned true (resp., false), we include $v(x)$ (resp., $v(\bar{x})$) in $\tilde{U}$, which makes a pre-assignment with size $k$. The bijection shown above guarantees that a $(3n+2m)$-vertex cover of $G_C$ containing $\tilde{U}$ is unique, that is, the pre-assignment is feasible.
439:For the only-if direction, suppose that $\tilde{U}$ is a feasible pre-assignment for $G_C$ with size at most $k$.
440:Let $U^*$ denote the unique minimum vertex cover of $G_C$ with $\tilde{U} \subseteq U^*$. Note that $\tilde{U}$ should be consistent with the conditions in the claim; for example, $\tilde{U}$ cannot include $v(x)$ and $v(\bar{x})$ simultaneously. 
441:If $\tilde{U}\subseteq V^X_C$, $\tilde{U}$ defines $k$ clues for $C$. Namely, if $v(x)\in \tilde{U}$ (resp., $v(\bar{x})\in \tilde{U}$), $x$ is assigned true (resp., false) in the clues, whose size becomes $k$.  Then the bijection guarantees the uniqueness of the solution under the clues.  
449:Also for this case, we show that $C$ has at most $k$ clues (i.e., a truth assignment for at most $k$ variables) such that the resulting $C$ has a unique solution if and only if $G_C$ has a feasible pre-assignment of size at most $k$. For the if-direction, suppose $X'$ is the set of $k$ variables. 
450:If $x\in X'$ is assigned true (resp., false), we exclude $v(\bar{x})$ (resp., $v({x})$) in $\hat{U}$, which makes a pre-assignment with size $k$. For this case also, the bijection shown above guarantees that $G_C$ has a unique $(3n+2m)$-vertex cover of $G_C$ that does not contain any vertex in $\hat{U}$, that is, the pre-assignment is feasible.
452:For the only-if direction, suppose that $\hat{U}$ is a feasible pre-assignment for $G_C$. Let $U^*$ denote the unique minimum vertex cover of $G_C$ with $U^* \subseteq V(G_C) \setminus \hat{U}$.
453:If $\hat{U}\subseteq V^X_C$, a similar argument to \textsc{Include} defines $k$ clues for $C$, which guarantees the uniqueness of the solution. We then consider the case when $\hat{U}$ contains a vertex outside of $V^X_C$. Such a vertex can be $r$ or one in $V_C^{X'} \cup V_C^{\mathrm{clause}}$. If $r\in \hat{U}$, we can remove it by condition \textbf{2} of the claim. If $v'(l)\in \hat{U}\cap V_C^{X'}$, where $l$ is a literal (i.e., $l=x$ or $l=\bar{x}$ for some $x$), we can replace $v'(l)$ by $v(l)$ in $\hat{U}$ by a similar argument to \textsc{Include} again. 
457:%\subsection{Bipartite Graphs}\label{sec:complexity:bipartite}
458:We consider the complexity of \pauvc on bipartite graphs.
460:For bipartite graphs, \textsc{Vertex Cover} is equivalent to the problem of finding a maximum cardinality matching by K\"{o}nig's theorem (see Chapter 2 in \cite{Diestel}), which can be computed in polynomial time by the Hopcroft-Karp algorithm~\cite{HopcroftK73}.
461:Combining this fact and \Cref{cor:unique_alg}, \pauvc belongs to NP under both models.
463:To see the NP-hardness of \pauvc, we first consider the \textsc{Mixed} model.
464:We perform a polynomial-time reduction from \textsc{Independent Dominating Set} on bipartite graphs.
466:This problem is NP-complete even on bipartite graphs~\cite{CorneilEt:IDS}.
